//! check: run(exit=nonzero)
//! phase: run
// Exercise 15-2: link is shared fate. Neither print runs; the linked
// failure takes main down at its blocking point. Silence, and a
// nonzero exit whose NUMBER belongs to the tool: `w.link()` from
// `main` couples `w` to the root supervisor's domain, and
// [conc.proc.root] terminates the process with "a nonzero,
// implementation-specified status" when that domain dies abnormally.
// lupin says 1, the compiled binary says 121, and [conf.trap.exit]
// rules that conforming tools compare the outcome class and never the
// number. This file read `run(exit=1)` until bs46 and was scored green
// on the compiler without ever compiling there (E0402 on the one-arg
// `link` until s157), which is how the wrong claim survived.
fn boom() -> !int { Bad }
fn main() -> !int {
    let w = spawn proc boom()
    w.link()
    let ch = channel[int](0)
    let v = ch.recv() else |err| { print("recv failed"); return 7 }
    print("got {v}")
    0
}
